1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
LCEL

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:07,000
In this lesson, we'll introduce LaneChain Expression Language, or LCEL.

3
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:10,000
This is a new syntax that makes it much easier

4
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:14,000
and more transparent to construct and work with different chains and agents.

5
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:16,000
Let's get to it.

6
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:18,000
A lot of the power of LaneChain

7
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:22,000
comes from composing chains of different components together.

8
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:24,000
And we have a new way of doing this.

9
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:28,000
And this is LCEL and a runnable protocol which define a few things.

10
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:32,000
First, it defines an allowed set of input types

11
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:35,000
and a corresponding allowed set of output types.

12
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:39,000
There's then a bunch of standard methods, and we'll go over all of these.

13
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:43,000
But these are standard methods that all runnables will expose.

14
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:45,000
So you can call them all in the same way.

15
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:48,000
There are ways of modifying parameters at runtime

16
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:50,000
and adding other options like fallbacks.

17
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:53,000
And you can do all of this, you can do all this composition

18
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:55,000
using the Linux pipe syntax.

19
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:57,000
So it'll look something like this.

20
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:02,000
What is this interface that we expect all runnables to expose?

21
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:05,000
First, there are a few common methods.

22
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:09,000
There's invoke, which calls the runnable on a single input.

23
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:14,000
There's stream, which calls it on a single input in stream's backer response.

24
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:18,000
And then there's batch, which calls it on a list of inputs.

25
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:22,000
For all of these synchronous methods, there's also a corresponding async method.

26
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:26,000
So there's a invoke, a stream, a batch.

27
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:31,000
There's common properties that all runnables have, namely an input schema and an output schema.

28
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:37,000
And you can see a list of the input and output types for common components below.

29
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:40,000
Why use LCEL?

30
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:42,000
There are a few benefits that this gets you.

31
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:47,000
First, you get async batch and streaming support out of the box.

32
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:51,000
So even if you're writing your code and testing it in a synchronous version,

33
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:58,000
you can easily port it over to production server that needs batch or needs async or needs streaming.

34
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:03,000
Second, a big part of working with LLMs is that they're a little bit unpredictable sometimes.

35
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:06,000
They don't always respond how you want them to respond.

36
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:11,000
And so you can easily attach fallbacks, not only to LLMs, but also to entire chains.

37
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:14,000
So you can have a fallback safety mechanism.

38
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:16,000
Third, parallelism.

39
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:18,000
LLM calls can be time consuming.

40
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:19,000
They often take a while.

41
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:22,000
And so it's really important to run them in parallel if you can.

42
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:26,000
And the LCEL syntax makes it easy to do that.

43
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:28,000
And fourth, logging is built in.

44
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:31,000
As these chains and agents get more and more complex,

45
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:38,000
being able to see the sequence of steps and the inputs and outputs becomes crucial for building an LLM application.

46
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:41,000
And we've added things into the open source library.

47
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:45,000
And then we also have Linksmith, which is a platform for logging and debugging steps.

48
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:51,000
And LCEL natively logs all of these things and all the inputs and outputs correspondingly.

49
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:55,000
With that, it's time to get to the code and see what this actually looks like.

50
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:59,000
First, let's set up our environment as before.

51
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:03,000
We'll then import a few different components that we're going to compose together.

52
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:19,000
First, we'll import a prompt template.

53
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:30,000
Next, we'll import a language model and we'll be using OpenAI.

54
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:32,000
And finally, we'll import an output parser.

55
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:34,000
And this is a really simple output parser.

56
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:37,000
It's just going to take a chat message and convert it into a string.

57
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:44,000
Let's now use these components to create a simple chain of prompt template to language model to output parser.

58
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:46,000
So first, let's create a prompt template.

59
00:03:46,000 --> 00:04:05,000
And we'll create one that asks the language model to tell us a short joke.

60
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:11,000
We'll then initialize the language model that we're going to use.

61
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:18,000
And finally, we'll create the output parser that we're going to use.

62
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:26,000
Now, to combine these all together into a chain, all we have to do is connect them with this pipe syntax.

63
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:32,000
And we can now call this chain using the invoke method on some inputs.

64
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:35,000
And the inputs here are going to be the inputs of the prompt template.

65
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:40,000
So that's going to be a dictionary with a keyword of topic.

66
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:42,000
And let's tell some joke about bears to start.

67
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:46,000
And so if we call this, we can see we get back a joke.

68
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:48,000
Why don't bears ever get caught in traffic?

69
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:51,000
Because they always take the beariest best routes.

70
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:56,000
Now's a good time to pause a little bit, pass in some other arguments to this function,

71
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:00,000
 maybe change up the prompt, play around with it and get a sense for these different components,

72
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:03,000
 and also combining them with this pipe syntax.

73
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:06,000
We'll now create a slightly more complex chain.

74
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:11,000
In the previous short course, we went over how to do retrieval augmented generation.

75
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:15,000
And so we'll replicate that same process using link chain expression language.

76
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:18,000
First, we need to set up our retriever.

77
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:20,000
We're first going to create a really simple vector store.

78
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:23,000
We're going to do this by initializing it with two texts.

79
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:25,000
First, harrisonwork.kencho.

80
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:27,000
And next, bears like dehoney.

81
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:30,000
We'll then also pass in an embedding model, open AI embeddings.

82
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:34,000
And then we'll create a retriever by doing vector store dot as retriever.

83
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:37,000
As a reminder from the previous lesson,

84
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:40,000
We can now call dot get relevant documents on this retriever.

85
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:42,000
And we get back relevant documents.

86
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:44,000
So we call it with where did Harrison work.

87
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:46,000
And we get back a list of documents.

88
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:49,000
And we can see that harrisonwork.kencho is the first one returned.

89
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:52,000
If we call it on something different.

90
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:56,000
If we call it on what do bears like to eat.

91
00:05:56,000 --> 00:06:03,000
We can see that the first result that we get back is bears like to eat honey.

92
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:06,000
So this is a simple example because there's only two documents.

93
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:08,000
And so it's returning both of them.

94
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:12,000
But the important thing is that you can do this on a larger number of documents.

95
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:14,000
And it will return the most relevant documents,

96
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:18,000
which we will then use in our retrieval augmented generation pipeline.

97
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:23,000
In order to create this pipeline we're first going to create a prompt.

98
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:28,000
That basically asks the language model to answer the question based only on the following context.

99
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:31,000
And has two variables here has context and question.

100
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:33,000
And so we're going to be passing in two things.

101
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:36,000
We're going to be passing in the question, which is going to be the user question.

102
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:38,000
And then we're going to be passing in the context.

103
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:40,000
And these are going to be the documents that we fetch from the retriever.

104
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:43,000
So how are we going to think about creating this chain?

105
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:48,000
We want the first and only input to the chain to be the user question.

106
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:51,000
From then we want to fetch relevant context.

107
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:53,000
And then we want to pass that into the prompt.

108
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:55,000
And then we want to pass that into a model.

109
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:59,000
And then we want to pass that into the output parser and convert the chat message into a string.

110
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:04,000
So the first thing that we're going to do is create something that takes in a single question.

111
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:09,000
And then turns it into a dictionary of two elements context and question.

112
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:22,000
To do that we're going to use a runnable map, which we can import this way.

113
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:24,000
We want this runnable map to have two elements.

114
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:26,000
First is going to be context.

115
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:28,000
And the context is going to call the retriever.

116
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:47,000
We can write a lambda function that calls retriever.getRelevantDocuments and passes in the question parameter.

117
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:51,000
We also want to pass along the original question parameter.

118
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:55,000
And we can do that by just using another simple lambda function.

119
00:07:55,000 --> 00:08:00,000
From here we want to pass it into the prompt.

120
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:02,000
And then we want to pass it in to the model.

121
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:04,000
And then we want to pass it into the output parser.

122
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:07,000
And so we can just compose all those together with pipes.

123
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:11,000
We can then name this as our chain.

124
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:13,000
And we now have a new runnable.

125
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:25,000
And now if we invoke this with a question like where did Harrison work?

126
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:28,000
We can see that it responds with Harrison worked at Kensho.

127
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:32,000
And we want to take a closer look at what exactly is going on under the scenes.

128
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:35,000
We can just look at this runnable map directly.

129
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:39,000
So let's create this as its own variable called inputs.

130
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:43,000
And then if we just run this on the same thing.

131
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:50,000
We can see that we get back context which is a list of documents.

132
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:52,000
And then the question as well.

133
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:53,000
So that's both elements there.

134
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:55,000
This is then getting passed into the prompt.

135
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:59,000
Which is then creating a prompt value which is getting passed into the model.

136
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:00,000
Which calls the model.

137
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:02,000
Returns a chat message which gets passed to the output parser.

138
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:04,000
And that finally returns a string.

139
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:09,000
One of the other things that we can do with runnables is we can bind parameters to them.

140
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:14,000
So we'll do that with an example of open AI functions which we just covered.

141
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:19,000
So if we have this list of functions we want to call the language model with those functions.

142
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:26,000
And so normally this would look like a prompt and then a model.

143
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:28,000
But where do the functions go in?

144
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:34,000
And so in order to insert the functions what we can do is we can do bind and then we can do functions equals functions.

145
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:41,000
And what this will do under the hood is that when the model is called it will pass any parameters and bind along to the invocation.

146
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:45,000
So now if we create a runnable by doing prompt and then pipe model.

147
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:55,000
When we call the runnable on some inputs that should use the function.

148
00:09:55,000 --> 00:10:02,000
Like what is the weather in SF.

149
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:08,000
We can see that what we get back is an AI message which has this additional quards parameter.

150
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:13,000
And in there we see the function call that we expect to be returned.

151
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:16,000
If you wanted to change that you would just override it.

152
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:22,000
So if we add in another function and let's name this one sports search.

153
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:26,000
And it's going to search for.

154
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:38,000
It's going to search for news of recent sport events.

155
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:41,000
And it's going to take in a team name.

156
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:51,000
And it's going to take in a team name.

157
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:52,000
And now we have these two different functions here.

158
00:10:52,000 --> 00:11:04,000
We can just update the model by binding it with new functions.

159
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:25,000
And then when we call this new runnable which we're using the new model in.

160
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:27,000
And we call it with how did the Patriots do yesterday.

161
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:31,000
We can see that it now knows to use sports search.

162
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:36,000
One of the really powerful things about language and expression language is you can easily attach fallbacks.

163
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:40,000
Not only the individual components but to entire sequences.

164
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:45,000
So let's take a look at that when we're trying to get a language model to output JSON.

165
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:47,000
In order to create a situation where this is going to fail.

166
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:53,000
We'll actually use an older version of a language model from OpenAI that isn't quite as good.

167
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:03,000
And to do that we're going to import an OpenAI language model from Langchain.LLMs.

168
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:05,000
This is different than the chat models.

169
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:07,000
The chat models are newer types of models.

170
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:10,000
And so they're generally pretty good at things like outputting JSON.

171
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:22,000
But older models which is Langchain.LLMs generally aren't.

172
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:26,000
We'll now create a simple model and a simple chain.

173
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:30,000
In this simple model we're going to be setting temperature equals zero.

174
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:31,000
So we get some determinism.

175
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:35,000
And then we're also going to be using this model text DaVinci 001.

176
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:38,000
This is a really early version of an OpenAI model.

177
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:43,000
Our chain is just going to be calling this language model and then piping the result into JSON.loads.

178
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:48,000
This will break if the result of the language model isn't valid JSON.

179
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:51,000
Let's now create a challenge problem statement for the language model.

180
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:54,000
We're going to ask it to write three poems in a JSON blob.

181
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:58,000
Where each poem is a JSON blob of the title, author, and the first line.

182
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:02,000
This is a pretty challenging problem and we want it to respond in valid JSON.

183
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:06,000
And so if we run the simple model on this.

184
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:14,000
We can see that it responds with some structured format and it's kind of close.

185
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:20,000
But if we take this and we try to decode it with JSON.

186
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:23,000
We can see that we get a JSON decode error.

187
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:27,000
Because of what that model is outputting isn't actually valid JSON.

188
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:32,000
The new models from OpenAI are pretty good at outputting valid JSON.

189
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:38,000
So we're going to create a model called ChatOpenAI and then we're going to create a chain by piping that model with an output parser.

190
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:43,000
That's basically taking the chat message from the model and converting it into a string.

191
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:48,000
And then we're going to pipe that string into JSON.loads.

192
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:55,000
If we use the same challenge with this model, we can see that it correctly out splits valid JSON.

193
00:13:55,000 --> 00:14:01,000
In order to use fallbacks, what we can do is we can create a final chain and we can start with the simple chain.

194
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:08,000
And then we can add fallbacks by using with fallbacks.

195
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:12,000
In with fallbacks, we'll pass in a list of other runnables.

196
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:16,000
And basically what will happen is that first, the core runnable tries to get run.

197
00:14:16,000 --> 00:14:20,000
If an error is raised, then it goes through this list in order.

198
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:21,000
In this case, there's only one element.

199
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:23,000
But if there was more, we'd go through this in order.

200
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:32,000
And it would call those until it succeeds.

201
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:38,000
If we now call this chain on the challenge, we can see that it responds in the correct format.

202
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:46,000
So what happened was that it tried on the simple chain, it failed, and then it went back to the good chain that's using chat open AI, and it succeeds.

203
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:49,000
And so that's what we're getting back from this final chain.

204
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:53,000
Finally, let's talk about the interface that these runnables expose.

205
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:55,000
Let's go back to the chain that we had before.

206
00:14:55,000 --> 00:14:57,000
We're asking it to tell us a joke.

207
00:14:57,000 --> 00:15:01,000
And let's take a look at a few different elements of this interface.

208
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:04,000
The one that we were using from the get-go is invoke.

209
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:09,000
This is a synchronous method that calls it on one input.

210
00:15:09,000 --> 00:15:13,000
There's also batch, which is going to call it on a list of inputs.

211
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:19,000
So here we pass in two different inputs, one with bears, one with frogs, and we call chain.batch.

212
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:25,000
And under the hood, this is actually executing them in parallel as much as possible.

213
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:28,000
We can also stream back responses.

214
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:31,000
So chain.stream is going to return an iterable.

215
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:34,000
And we can loop over that iterable and print out the different elements.

216
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:39,000
So if we run this now, we can see the response streaming back to the user.

217
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:44,000
This is really important for LLM applications because sometimes LLM applications can take a while.

218
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:48,000
And so it's very, very nice to stream back some of these responses.

219
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:51,000
So it's not just an empty screen that the user is looking at.

220
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:56,000
And finally, all of these methods have corresponding asynchronous methods.

221
00:15:56,000 --> 00:16:00,000
So chain.ainvoke calls it an asynchronous manner.

222
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:03,000
And so that's why we have this await here because we want to see the response.

223
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:08,000
And this does the exact same thing as invoke, but it's just asynchronous.

224
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:09,000
That is it for this lesson.

225
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:18,000
But there's a lot of different ways that you can combine all these elements and put together really complicated types of chains and sequences.

226
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:24,000
We sometimes see sequences that get up to 300 or 400 components in a row.

227
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:29,000
And so before moving on, I would definitely take a moment to pause and try this out on some sequences of your own.

228
